---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { CONTACT_EMAIL } from '../lib/site-config';
---
<Base title="Child safety — Lrnon"
  description="How Lrnon protects children: no public posting until moderation exists, guardian consent for under-18 accounts and volunteers, and a named route for reporting harm.">
  <section class="container prose" style="padding-block:var(--sp-12);">
    <h1 style="font-size:var(--fs-500);">Child safety</h1>
    <p style="color:var(--c-ink-soft);">Version 1.0 · last updated 2026-08-24</p>

    <div class="note note--aim">
      <p style="margin:0;">
        <strong>The short version.</strong> Learning here is open to every age and collects
        no personal data at all. Nothing on Lrnon lets one learner send anything to another,
        and that stays true until moderation exists. If you are worried about a child in
        connection with Lrnon, write to <a href={'mailto:' + CONTACT_EMAIL}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>
        and say so in the subject line.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Why this page exists before the risk does</h2>
    <p>Lrnon has no public rooms, no comments, no direct messages and no way for
    one learner to reach another. The safest possible position, and the current
    one.</p>
    <p>The design for Lrnon includes topic rooms and a questions board. This
    page is written first, deliberately. A safeguarding policy produced after a
    feature ships is a reaction; produced before, it is a constraint on what may
    ship at all. What follows is that constraint.</p>

    <h2>What is open today</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Learning</strong> — every age, no account needed, no personal data collected.</li>
      <li><strong>Accounts</strong> — optional, for syncing progress across devices. Under 18 requires guardian consent.</li>
      <li><strong>Volunteering</strong> — open to under-18s with guardian consent, and we never publish their name or contact details.</li>
      <li><strong>Feedback</strong> — reaches one person by email. It is not published.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Nothing a learner writes is visible to another learner.</strong>
    There is currently no mechanism by which a stranger could contact a child
    through this site.</p>

    <h2>Conditions before any public posting opens</h2>
    <p>Rooms, a questions board, or any other learner-to-learner surface will
    not open until all of the following are true. This list is a commitment,
    not an aspiration.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>A named person is responsible</strong> for safeguarding, with
      a deputy, and the role appears on the <a href="/admin/people">roles page</a>
      rather than sitting in the unstaffed column.</li>
      <li><strong>Posts are moderated before or immediately after publication</strong>,
      with a queue somebody actually reads daily — not a report button and hope.</li>
      <li><strong>No private messaging, ever.</strong> Public rooms only. Grooming
      needs privacy; a site with no private channel removes the mechanism rather
      than trying to detect it.</li>
      <li><strong>No profile fields that identify a child</strong> — no age, school,
      location, photo or contact details. Display names are generated, not chosen.</li>
      <li><strong>Reporting is one click from every post</strong>, works without an
      account, and reaches a person rather than a queue nobody owns.</li>
      <li><strong>A written escalation route</strong>, including when to contact
      local authorities, decided before it is needed rather than during.</li>
      <li><strong>Retention and deletion rules</strong> for anything a child posts,
      published on this page.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Until every one of those holds, the <a href="/community">community page</a>
    marks these spaces as planned and they stay closed. A room nobody is watching
    is worse than no room.</p>

    <h2>Guardian consent</h2>
    <p>Where the law treats a person as a child, we ask a parent or guardian
    first. India's <abbr title="Digital Personal Data Protection Act 2023">DPDP
    Act</abbr> sets that at 18 and requires <em>verifiable</em> consent; the GDPR
    sets 13–16 depending on the country and COPPA covers under-13s in the US. We
    apply whichever is stricter for the person.</p>
    <p>Consent is a reply from the guardian's own email address — never a
    checkbox on a child's screen, which verifies nothing. It can be withdrawn at
    any time, immediately, with no reason required. The full mechanism is on the
    <a href="/privacy">privacy page</a>.</p>

    <h2>What we will never do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Show advertising to anyone, at any age.</li>
      <li>Profile or behaviourally target a child. DPDP prohibits it; we would not want it regardless.</li>
      <li>Sell, share or license learner data.</li>
      <li>Publish a child's name, photograph, school or location — even with consent.</li>
      <li>Use anything a learner writes to train a model.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Sky, and why it is off</h2>
    <p>Sky is the site assistant. It is currently disabled and will not reach
    learners until it has been reviewed by hand at length. When it does run it
    answers only from Lrnon's own pages, refuses medical, legal, exam-authority,
    financial and personal-safety questions outright, and hands those to a
    person instead. It never asks for a name, phone number, school or payment
    details.</p>

    <h2>Reporting a concern</h2>
    <p>Write to <a href={'mailto:' + CONTACT_EMAIL}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> with
    <strong>"child safety"</strong> in the subject. One person reads it, and
    it is treated ahead of everything else.</p>
    <p>If a child is in immediate danger, contact your local emergency services
    first. We are a learning website; we are not an emergency service and will
    not pretend otherwise.</p>

    <div class="note note--try">
      <p style="margin:0;">
        <strong>This policy is not legal advice and was not written by a lawyer.</strong>
        It states what Lrnon does and what it will not ship without. Before public posting
        opens, it should be reviewed by someone qualified in the jurisdictions Lrnon
        operates in.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
